{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied PostgreSQL stock schema as branch_stock on node1 and preserve its supplied records and source file.","status":"satisfied","summary":"branch_stock contains the supplied stock and supplier_costs tables and all four records after restart. The source SQL retains its baseline SHA-256 checksum on both nodes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d7feb51eb8544689","cmd-928494a06e30470b","cmd-3bc0e6457da6411b","cmd-b9f452ca19df4c8b","seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow stock_app from node2 to read stock and update quantities, but not alter schema, update non-quantity fields, or read supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Remote behavioral tests show stock reads and a successful quantity update, rolled back to preserve records. Supplier-cost reads, SKU updates, and table creation are denied; the final privilege matrix corroborates these restrictions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4f1226399b544130","cmd-055ff52cc2cf4428","cmd-30dedb95aea94f3d","cmd-3bc0e6457da6411b"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Give auditor read-only access to stock and supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The auditor reads both tables remotely, while update and schema-creation attempts are denied. Final privilege inspection shows SELECT on both tables and no stock UPDATE privilege.","evidence_ids":["cmd-28ac7eacb9e643c1","cmd-30dedb95aea94f3d","cmd-3bc0e6457da6411b"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Establish separate credentials for stock_app and auditor.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Independent passwords were generated for distinct login roles, and separate stock_app.pgpass and auditor.pgpass files are present on node2 as root-owned mode-600 files, including after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d7feb51eb8544689","cmd-ad720c13f0dc4455","cmd-f513f18259284fda","cmd-30dedb95aea94f3d","cmd-b9f452ca19df4c8b"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remain AlmaLinux 9.6 on kernel 5.14.0-570.12.1.el9_6 after execution and restart. PostgreSQL 13 was installed from the platform's DNF repositories, remained operational after restart, and package-health checks passed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-71a6f86e4b4a4131","cmd-bf4a9b78e2bf47c9","cmd-1e997eaed0ae470c","cmd-1428fe02616c4d06","cmd-3bc0e6457da6411b","cmd-b9f452ca19df4c8b","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"The implementation is focused and healthy: PostgreSQL exposure is limited to node1's managed address with node2-specific authentication and firewall rules; both nodes have no failed services and healthy package state. The provider restart protocol is complete for both nodes, with healthy pre-restart states, acknowledgements, changed boot identities independently corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, data, credentials, and role behavior. One unnecessary mutation was identified in cmd-186a6425f6e64302: node1 appended the cluster public key to its own root authorized_keys, which had no role in node1's outgoing credential transfer. No later command reverted that low-risk but security-relevant authorization change. The private key installation on node1 and node2 authorization in cmd-571afb4779ac41e5 were causally used for the credential transfer.","evidence_ids":["cmd-186a6425f6e64302","cmd-571afb4779ac41e5","cmd-f9e697f86248407e","cmd-6775204c87bc4be8","cmd-fde874f14b8c4ded","cmd-928494a06e30470b","cmd-f513f18259284fda","cmd-9148cccf630b4254","cmd-508da9e0bd4249d1","cmd-30dedb95aea94f3d","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and behavioral evidence. The supplied database records and files were preserved, both roles enforce the requested least-privilege behavior from node2, credentials are separate and protected, and the legacy OS and kernel remain unchanged. Both mandatory node-restart protocols were successfully completed and independently corroborated.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /root/.ssh, so the final presence of the unnecessary node1 authorized_keys entry is inferred from the complete command timeline showing its creation and no cleanup.","Credential contents were appropriately redacted; separation is supported by independent generation commands and distinct protected files rather than direct comparison of the secret values."]}
